Step 1: Add nullable column with no default:
ALTER TABLE apprentices ADD COLUMN tier TEXT;
Lock: brief ACCESS EXCLUSIVE (milliseconds — no data rewrite)

Step 2: Backfill in batches to avoid long locks:
UPDATE apprentices SET tier = 'APPRENTICE' WHERE tier IS NULL AND id BETWEEN 1 AND 100000;
-- Repeat in batches of 100k until done
No lock: each batch UPDATE is a normal write

Step 3: Add DEFAULT for future inserts:
ALTER TABLE apprentices ALTER COLUMN tier SET DEFAULT 'APPRENTICE';
Brief lock — no data rewrite

Step 4: Add NOT NULL constraint — requires a full table scan to verify:
ALTER TABLE apprentices ALTER COLUMN tier SET NOT NULL;
PostgreSQL 12+: can use a CHECK CONSTRAINT NOT VALID to skip scan, then VALIDATE later
OR: trust the backfill and add directly — brief lock for statistics update

Best approach on PG12+:
ALTER TABLE apprentices ADD CONSTRAINT tier_not_null CHECK (tier IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID;
-- No lock, skips validation
ALTER TABLE apprentices VALIDATE CONSTRAINT tier_not_null;
-- Acquires ShareUpdateExclusiveLock — allows reads/writes during validation
